# Packages to install, read by bin/system/pack.
#
# One entry per line: one or more alternatives separated by |, in order of
# preference. The first alternative whose manager this machine has, and which
# can supply the package, installs it. Each alternative is MANAGER:NAME, or
# just MANAGER to take the name of the next alternative that has one. By
# convention, " | " separates alternatives naming different packages:
#   apt:fd-find | brew|scoop:fd
#
# Managers:
# - apt     | Debian-based packages
# - brew    | Homebrew bottles; TAP/NAME for a tap other than core
# - cask    | Homebrew GUI apps (macOS); TAP/NAME for a tap other than core
# - scoop   | Windows CLI tools; BUCKET/NAME for a bucket other than main
# - winget  | Windows apps, by package ID, including Microsoft Store ones
# - npm     | tools from the JavaScript world
# - uv      | tools from the Python world
# - flatpak | Linux desktop apps from Flathub, by app ID
# - custom  | runs the script bin/packages/install-NAME
#
# Each custom script must support --check, exiting 0 when its tool is
# installed and current. A "# Platforms:" line in its header (linux, macos,
# freebsd, windows) limits the OSes where it counts as an alternative.
# An npm entry followed by the word scripts may run its install scripts,
# which npm otherwise warns about, and will someday refuse to run.
# A uv entry followed by python=VERSION installs its tool on that Python,
# e.g. when the newest Python lacks wheels the tool needs.
# An apt entry followed by ppa=OWNER/NAME needs that Launchpad PPA, and one
# followed by repo=NAME needs the repository that bin/packages/repo-NAME sets
# up (supporting --check, as custom scripts do); pack sets up either first.
# Any entry may be followed by the word gui, to install it only on desktops.
# Any entry may be followed by replaces=MANAGER:NAME[,MANAGER:NAME...],
# naming packages it supersedes when another of its alternatives installs it,
# e.g. distro packages too old to use; pack warns about any of
# those still installed, and suggests how to remove them.
#
# [sections]: core always installs. The rest are opt-in, via --with SECTION
# or the machine's profile file (~/.config/dotfiles/sections, written by
# setup.sh). A section header may name sections it requires, e.g. [dev: build].

